From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Kocielinski <daniel.koc@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 17:27:57 -0400
Subject: [sandbox] Restore ExternalString resource_data_ after serialization

Serializer::ObjectSerializer::SerializeExternalString() replaces an
external string's resource_ field with an encoded external reference
index for the duration of serialization, and restores it afterwards
because the string stays live in the serializing isolate (that is the
case for CodeSerializer, i.e. ScriptCompiler::CreateCodeCache()).

Under V8_ENABLE_SANDBOX the restore was done by calling
SetResourceRefForSerialization() a second time, with the saved handle.
That method however also nulls out the resource_data_ handle - which is
what the serialized form needs, but not what a restore should do - so
the live string was left with a null cached data pointer handle. The
non-sandbox path does not have this problem, it restores through
set_address_as_resource(), which updates both fields.

Any later read of the cached data pointer from generated code then loads
external pointer table entry 0, whose tag does not match
kExternalStringResourceDataTag, and fails the CSA_SBXCHECK in
CodeStubAssembler::LoadExternalPointerFromObject(). Embedders that
externalize strings they also pass to the compiler - e.g. builtin module
sources that are subsequently turned into a code cache - hit this as a
process abort on the next String.prototype.startsWith()/indexOf() (or
any other builtin going through ToDirectStringAssembler) on such a
string.

Save and restore both handles instead. The external pointer table
entries are not freed while the string is serialized, only the in-object
handle fields are overwritten, so re-storing the saved handles re-links
the string with its existing entries.

Introduced in commit 8137040c3a7 ("[sandbox] Sandboxify ExternalString
external pointers").

